@@ -0,0 +1,471 @@
+---
+name: intent
+position: both
+description: 'Turn whatever a person shares to explain what they want — a prototype (a file, a project folder or a deployed site), a document, or a plain statement — into two levels of intent: the person''s Business Intents (what they want for the business, with why it matters, who asked, and how we will know it is met, each confirmed or dropped by them) and, under each, the ICE the source shows (the separate things they must see, decide or do, as goals, not yet placed under a capability). Keeps what they shared as Sources, exactly as they were when read, and links intents, ICE and Sources. The product model is the hand-off to later plays. Both are kinds of the product ontology. The first play of the Kickoff drive, and the hand-in from a person to the product model: /vision starts from its result. Run by hand it opens and lands its own change; handed a drive''s JSON contract it opens nothing of its own.'
+user-invocable: true
+---
+
+# intent
+
+Turn what a person shares into two levels of intent, and keep what they shared as its
+**Source**:
+
+- **Business Intents** — the person's level: what they want for the business, in their words,
+  with why it matters and how we will know it is met. They sit one "why?" above what a source
+  shows, and mostly come from the person. The person confirms or drops each; nothing else does.
+- **ICE** — the agents' level, under each intent: one per separate thing the source lets the
+  person see, decide or do, as goals. Saved in the `ice.yaml` shape, not yet placed under a
+  capability; `/vision` and later plays place it and add to it.
+
+All are kinds of the product ontology (`standards/schemas/product-os/ontology.md`), and the
+product model is the hand-off. No play owns a kind; drift is fixed by alignment (ontology v3).
+
+The source is the input, never the intent. A prototype or a document shows one way to build
+what the person wants; this play pulls out the *want* and leaves the *way* behind (IDD
+Principle 1).
+
+**Pipeline position: both.** Run by hand, the D2 rule prepends `start-change` and appends
+`commit-change → propose-change → review-change → merge-change`, so `/intent` opens and lands
+its own change. **Inside a drive** the drive hands it a JSON contract (`--contract`); then it
+opens no issue or branch of its own, skips the end sequence, and writes on the drive's branch
+(ADR 030 P4; #612 D3).
+
+## Compiled From
+
+This play was compiled from the intent ICE (`reference/ice.md`) by play-creator (#612).
+Intent defines constraints (C1–C13) and failure conditions (F1–F13); the expectation defines
+success scenarios (S1–S8), a Done means (D1–D5, baked to `stop-condition.yaml`), and one
+recovery entry per failure condition. To modify this play, update `reference/ice.md` and
+recompile with play-editor.
+Do NOT edit this file manually — it is a compiled artifact.
+
+## Role
+
+You are the orchestrator. You own the step order and the one human checkpoint. You run the
+mechanical work through bundled scripts — capturing the sources, checking the draft, saving
+the intents and their ICE, guarding the model — and you hand the one piece of judgment,
+drafting both levels from the sources, to the `business-intent-keeper` agent, which invokes
+the `author-business-intent` skill and checks the draft, through a JSON contract over files
+on disk. You ask the person every question the sources cannot answer, each with example
+answers, and only their typed reply confirms or drops each intent.
+
+**Forbidden:** confirming or dropping an intent yourself or through any agent (C4); filling a
+property the source does not show and the person did not say (C5); describing the source
+inside an intent or an ICE goal (C3); merging separate things into one ICE, leaving a part
+unmapped, or saving an ICE with no business intent (C12); asking why or proof with no example
+answers (C13); writing any model file other than the new intents and ICE (C8); opening an issue or a branch
+while running inside a drive (C9); closing COMPLETED without the stop condition held (C11).
+
+**Agent boundaries:**
+
+| Agent | Domain | Skill it invokes | Phases |
+|-------|--------|------------------|--------|
+| `business-intent-keeper` | Read every Source's snapshot and the person's answers; draft the business intents and, under each, one ICE per separate thing the sources show — with provenance, a coverage map, and questions with example answers — and check the draft (coverage, merges and restated intents by judgment, the rest by script) before handing it back | `author-business-intent` | Draft |
+
+`business-intent-keeper` is the single **domain agent**. It drafts both levels because one
+reading of the source shows both; it never writes the model.
+The start and end sequence are injected
+sub-plays. Everything else is a script.
+
+## Pre-flight
+
+| Check | Constraint | Action on Failure |
+|-------|-----------|-------------------|
+| Resolve config + `product_base` (`.garura/core/config.yaml`) | — | Hard halt |
+| A source was given: a file path, a project folder, an http(s) link, or the text of a statement | C1 | Ask the person for it (gather, not a halt) |
+| Drive contract: when `--contract <file>` is passed, it is valid JSON naming `drive_run_id` and `branch`, and the current branch equals that branch | C9 | Hard halt (REC9) |
+| **Clean model tree** — after Step 0 (or at once, inside a drive), `git status --porcelain -- <product_base>product-os` is empty | C8 | Hard halt (REC8) |
+
+Resolve the facts with the bundled resolver — not by inference:
+
+```
+git branch --show-current
+git status --porcelain > <working>/porcelain.txt
+python3 scripts/preflight.py --play intent --config .garura/core/config.yaml \
+    --branch "<current branch>" --porcelain-file <working>/porcelain.txt
+python3 scripts/session_stamp.py --phase start \
+    --marker "{stm_base}_kickoff/intent/status/session-stamp-intent.json" \
+    --cwd "$(pwd)" --branch "$(git branch --show-current)"
+```
+
+`in_drive` is true exactly when a valid `--contract` was passed; record it in
+`<working>/run.json` with the contract's `drive_run_id` and `branch`. The run's working folder
+`<working>` is `{stm_base}_kickoff/intent/<run-ts>/`; the stop condition reads it there. Each
+Source's snapshot goes to the evidence folder `{product_base}_evidence/intent/<run-ts>/source-<name>/`
+— product-scoped, so it outlives the run.
+
+## Task DAG
+
+Create ALL tasks immediately after pre-flight, before any domain work. Inside a drive, T0 and
+E1–E4 are created and marked skipped (`in_drive`), never run.
+
+```
+[T0] start-change (injected — start; skipped in a drive)   blockedBy: []
+[T1] Capture the sources                                  blockedBy: [T0]
+[T2] Draft the intents and their ICE                      blockedBy: [T1]
+[T3] Ask and check (≤3 rounds)                            blockedBy: [T2]
+[T4] Confirm or drop each intent (pinned)                 blockedBy: [T3]
+[T5] Save the intents and ICE, link the sources           blockedBy: [T4]
+[T6] Guard the model                                      blockedBy: [T5]
+[T7] Commit the delta                                     blockedBy: [T6]
+[E1] commit-change (injected — end; skipped in a drive)   blockedBy: [T7]
+[E2] propose-change (injected — end; skipped in a drive)  blockedBy: [E1]
+[E3] review-change (injected — end; skipped in a drive)   blockedBy: [E2]
+[E4] merge-change (injected — end; skipped in a drive)    blockedBy: [E3]
+[T8] Scenario Validation                                  blockedBy: [E4]
+[T9] Close                                                blockedBy: [T8]
+```
+
+Mark each task in progress before its step and completed right after its eval passes. No
+runtime reordering. On resume, skip completed and reset in-progress to pending.
+
+## Workflow
+
+### Phase: Start (injected — D2 position: start)
+
+**Step 0 — start-change** · Owner: `start-change` (sub-play) · Depends on: pre-flight
+*Skipped when `in_drive`.* Run the start-of-pipeline member as a sub-play with
+`parent_run_id`: it resolves or creates the issue, cuts the branch off fresh main, and inits
+STM. Then run the clean-tree assertion.
+
+    { "play": "start-change", "parent_run_id": "<this run id>",
+      "inputs": { "title": "intent: <a few words of what the person wants>" },
+      "outputs": { "result": "{stm_base}_kickoff/intent/start/start-change.json" } }
+
+### Phase: Capture
+
+**Step 1 — Capture the sources** · Owner: play (script) · Depends on: Step 0
+Keep each source exactly as it was when read — one capture per Source, each with its own
+`<name>`. For a statement, first write the person's exact words to `<working>/statement.txt`.
+The given-by is the git user running the play (`git config user.name` and `user.email`):
+
+```
+python3 scripts/capture_source.py \
+    ( --source <file path or link> [--also <more pages>] [--click <tab label> ...] \
+      | --statement <working>/statement.txt ) \
+    --out-dir {product_base}_evidence/intent/<run-ts>/source-<name> \
+    --given-by "<git user.name> <<git user.email>>" \
+    --manifest <working>/source-manifest-<name>.json
+```
+
+A deployed site is run through in a headless browser and captured page by page — when the
+app switches views with tabs or buttons on one page, name each with `--click` so every view is
+captured, not only the first. Every view keeps its picture and its visible text. An HTML file
+is copied and rendered; a document is copied; a statement is kept byte for byte. A **project
+folder** is two Sources: capture the folder (its written docs — README, SPEC — are kept), then
+start the project the way its README says, capture the running app as a `site` Source, and
+stop it. Both Sources link to every intent the run confirms.
+**SE-1 (F1/C1):** every capture exited 0 and each `source-manifest-<name>.json` reads
+`snapshot_saved: true` with a `kind`; a source that could not be read (or run through) halted
+the run (REC1).
+**SE-6 (F6/C6):** each `source-manifest-<name>.json` lists the snapshot files with their
+hashes, `read_on`, and `given_by` (the git user); each snapshot sits under the evidence folder.
+
+### Phase: Draft
+
+**Step 2 — Draft the intents and their ICE** · Owner: `business-intent-keeper` · Depends on: Step 1
+
+    { "task":    "draft the person's Business Intents and their ICE from these sources and their answers",
+      "inputs":  { "source_manifests": ["<working>/source-manifest-<name>.json", "<one per Source>"],
+                   "answers": "<working>/answers.yaml (absent on the first pass)",
+                   "ontology_path": "<memory>/standards/schemas/product-os/ontology.md",
+                   "check_script": "<play-dir>/scripts/check_intent.py" },
+      "outputs": { "draft": "<working>/intent-draft.yaml",
+                   "check_report": "<working>/intent-check.json" },
+      "task_id": "draft-intent" }
+
+The agent invokes `author-business-intent`, which writes the draft: one ICE per separate thing
+the sources show, as plain goals; above them the business intents they serve, found by asking
+"why?" until the answer no longer names the product (each property with a provenance,
+`source` or `person`; an intent the person stated may have no ICE yet); what each source shows;
+the coverage map from every part of a source to its ICE; and the questions the sources cannot
+answer, each tagged with its intent and carrying up to three example answers. The agent then
+checks the draft — every page, tab and section mapped, nothing merged, no intent that restates
+the source, by judgment; the rest by the check script — and sends problems back to the skill
+(at most twice). It returns only its JSON contract: `completed`, or `needs_person` when only
+the person's questions are left, with both paths and the counts.
+
+### Phase: Ask and check
+
+**Step 3 — Ask and check** · Owner: play (script + the person) · Depends on: Step 2 · ≤3 rounds
+Check the draft mechanically:
+
+```
+python3 scripts/check_intent.py --draft <working>/intent-draft.yaml \
+    --source-manifest <working>/source-manifest-<name>.json [--source-manifest <one per Source>] \
+    [--answers <working>/answers.yaml] \
+    --source-text <a snapshot's readable text: statement.txt, a document, or a view's .txt> \
+    [--source-text <one more per readable snapshot file, across every Source>] \
+    --out <working>/intent-check.json
+```
+
+For every open question and every missing property, ask the person — plainly, one at a time,
+naming the intent it belongs to, with the question's example answers (pick one, change one, or
+write your own). Record each reply in `<working>/answers.yaml` exactly:
+`{ about: <intent> — <why|proof|…>, reply: <their words>, offered: [<the examples>], picked: true }`
+— `picked: true` only when the reply is one of the examples. Something the person says
+unprompted (an intent they add, a correction) is recorded as `volunteered: true`, with no
+examples. Then re-dispatch Step 2. For any
+other problem the check found (a copied run of words, code-like text, a bare label, a part
+mapped to no ICE, an ICE with no intent), re-dispatch Step 2 with the check report. Stop when the check is clean; after 3 rounds still unclean, halt and show the person
+what is left.
+**SE-2 (F2/C2):** the final `intent-check.json` reports, for every intent, no missing property
+and no bare label.
+**SE-3 (F3/C3):** it reports no copied run from the source and no code-like text.
+**SE-4 (F5/C5):** it reports every filled property's provenance as `source` or `person`, and
+no unanswered question.
+**SE-12 (F12/C12):** it reports a coverage map with no part mapped to no ICE and no ICE mapped
+from nothing, every ICE built from an existing intent, every intent the source shows with at
+least one ICE, every Source summarised; the agent's contract reads `completed` (its judgment
+found no unmapped page, tab or section, no merged ICE, and no intent that restates the source).
+**SE-13 (F13/C13):** it reports every question carrying 1–3 example answers, and — run with
+`--answers` — every recorded answer listing the examples it was offered, with every reply that
+equals an example marked `picked: true`.
+
+### Phase: Confirm (pinned human checkpoint)
+
+**Step 4 — Confirm or drop each intent** · Owner: play · Depends on: Step 3 · **pinned — never
+skipped by config or policy, inside a drive or not**
+First show what each source shows and the coverage map — every part of the sources, the ICE
+it serves, and the intent that ICE is built from — so the person sees nothing was left out or
+merged. The person may type **missing: <what>** or **merged: <item>** here; add it to
+`answers.yaml` and go back to Step 2. Then show each intent, one at a time, exactly as it will
+read — title, outcome, why, asked by, proof it is met, must not — with the ICE under it and
+their goals, and ask the person to type one of: **confirm**, **drop**, **change: <what>**, or
+**stop**. Confirming an intent keeps its ICE; dropping it drops its ICE.
+- **confirm** / **drop** → record the decision with their exact words; go to the next intent.
+- **change** → add the change to `answers.yaml` and go back to Step 2 (decisions already
+  recorded stand unless the change touches that intent).
+- **stop** → save nothing, and close HALTED with the reason (S4).
+When every intent is decided, write `<working>/confirmation.yaml`:
+`confirmation: { by: person, confirmed_by: <git user>, at: <date>, decisions: [ { intent: <id>, decision: confirmed | dropped, reply: <their exact words> } ] }`.
+**SE-5 (F4/C4):** `confirmation.yaml` reads `by: person`, holds one decision for every
+drafted intent, each with the person's own non-empty reply, and was written only after each
+intent was shown to them; no agent wrote or edited it.
+
+### Phase: Save
+
+**Step 5 — Save the intents and ICE, link the sources** · Owner: play (script) · Depends on: Step 4
+
+```
+python3 scripts/persist_intent.py --draft <working>/intent-draft.yaml \
+    --confirmation <working>/confirmation.yaml \
+    --source-manifest <working>/source-manifest-<name>.json [--source-manifest <one per Source>] \
+    --product-base <product_base> --working <working>
+```
+
+It writes `<product_base>product-os/intents/<id>.md` for each confirmed intent and
+`<product_base>product-os/ice/<id>.yaml` for each ICE built from one (the `ice.yaml` shape,
+goals only, `node_ref` empty, `built_from` its intent) — never over an existing file; a dropped
+intent and its ICE are not saved. It writes each Source record `source.md` beside its snapshot,
+links intents, ICE and Sources, and writes `intent-manifest.json`. It writes no hand-off file:
+later plays read the model. It refuses, and writes nothing, unless the person decided every
+intent and confirmed at least one.
+**SE-7 (F7/C7):** `intent-manifest.json` reads `linked: true`; each intent page names every
+Source and its ICE, each ICE names its intent, and each `source.md` names every confirmed
+intent.
+**SE-10 (F10/C10):** every confirmed intent has its page and every ICE built from it has its
+file in the `ice.yaml` shape under the product model; no `vision-goal` file exists.
+
+**Step 6 — Guard the model** · Owner: play (script) · Depends on: Step 5
+
+```
+python3 scripts/scoped_write_guard.py --product-base <product_base> --base-ref HEAD \
+    --add-only 'product-os/intents/*.md' --add-only 'product-os/ice/*.yaml' \
+    --out <working>/guard-report.json
+```
+
+On a non-zero exit, re-run with `--restore` and apply REC8.
+**SE-8 (F8/C8):** `guard-report.json` reads `ok: true` — only new intent pages and new ICE files
+were added under the product model.
+
+**Step 7 — Commit the delta** · Owner: play · Depends on: Step 6
+Make the intents, their ICE and their Sources durable on the branch (the drive's branch inside
+a drive):
+
+```
+git add -- <product_base>product-os/intents <product_base>product-os/ice <product_base>_evidence/intent/<run-ts>
+git commit -m "feat(model): business intents — <n> from <what the source is> (#<issue>)"
+```
+
+### Phase: End sequence (injected — D2 position: end)
+
+*Skipped when `in_drive` — the drive lands its own change.* After the commit, the D2 rule
+injects the close sequence, each a sub-play with `parent_run_id`:
+
+**Step E1 — commit-change** · blockedBy: Step 7
+
+    { "play": "commit-change", "parent_run_id": "<this run id>", "inputs": {}, "outputs": { "result": "{stm_base}_kickoff/intent/end/commit-change.json" } }
+
+**Step E2 — propose-change** · blockedBy: E1
+
+    { "play": "propose-change", "parent_run_id": "<this run id>", "inputs": {}, "outputs": { "result": "{stm_base}_kickoff/intent/end/propose-change.json" } }
+
+**Step E3 — review-change** · blockedBy: E2
+
+    { "play": "review-change", "parent_run_id": "<this run id>", "inputs": {}, "outputs": { "result": "{stm_base}_kickoff/intent/end/review-change.json" } }
+
+**Step E4 — merge-change** · blockedBy: E3
+
+    { "play": "merge-change", "parent_run_id": "<this run id>", "inputs": {}, "outputs": { "result": "{stm_base}_kickoff/intent/end/merge-change.json" } }
+
+Each end member owns its own evals; a review reject stops the chain before merge; the land on
+main keeps its pinned human approval.
+**SE-9 (F9/C9):** by hand, `start-change` ran at the head and `merge-change.json` reports the
+PR merged; inside a drive, `run.json` reads `in_drive: true`, neither `start-change` nor any
+end member ran, and the branch the commit landed on equals the contract's branch.
+
+### Phase: Scenario Validation
+
+**Step 8 — Scenario evals** · Owner: play · Depends on: the end sequence (or Step 7 in a drive)
+- **SCE-1 (S1 — business owner, deployed prototype):** `intent-manifest.json` reads
+  `any_confirmed: true`, `all_decided: true` and `confirmed_by`; every required property is on
+  each intent page; the site's `source-manifest-<name>.json` lists at least one screenshot;
+  the manifest reads `linked: true`; the guard reads ok; `merge-change.json` reports the PR
+  merged.
+- **SCE-2 (S2 — founder, plain statement):** the `source-manifest-<name>.json` reads `kind: statement`;
+  `statement.txt` in the snapshot matches the person's words byte for byte (its hash matches);
+  `intent-check.json` reports no copied run.
+- **SCE-3 (S3 — business owner, nothing invented):** every filled property of every intent has
+  provenance `source` or `person`, none empty or `agent`, and every question the source could
+  not answer carries the person's answer.
+- **SCE-4 (S4 — business owner, does not confirm):** when the person typed **stop**, or
+  dropped every intent, no intent page exists, `persist_intent.py` refused, the model paths are
+  clean, and the run closed HALTED with the reason.
+- **SCE-5 (S5 — Kickoff drive):** `run.json` reads `in_drive: true`; no `start-change` or end
+  result file exists for this run; the commit is on the contract's branch.
+- **SCE-6 (S6 — product strategist, hand-over through the model):** each ICE file is in the
+  `ice.yaml` shape with goals, `node_ref` empty and `built_from` naming its intent; each intent
+  page names its ICE; no `vision-goal` file exists.
+- **SCE-7 (S7 — business owner, a prototype that does several things):** the draft holds one
+  ICE per separate thing; the coverage map names every part and an existing ICE for each;
+  every ICE names an existing intent; every intent with no ICE was stated by the person;
+  `confirmation.yaml` holds a decision for every drafted intent.
+- **SCE-8 (S8 — business owner, answering why and proof):** every answer in `answers.yaml`
+  lists the examples it was offered, and every reply that equals an example is marked
+  `picked: true`.
+
+### Phase: Evidence & Close
+
+**Step 9 — Close** · Owner: play · Depends on: Step 8
+Run the Standard Play Close. `/intent` writes product-scoped evidence beside the Sources'
+snapshots, so the record of the run and what it read stay together.
+**SE-11 (F11/C11):** the close is stop-condition gated — `check_stop_condition.py` over the
+baked `stop-condition.yaml` (D1 intents saved; D2 at least one confirmed and every one decided;
+D3 every Source snapshot saved; D4 intents, ICE and Sources linked; D5 guard ok) reads **held** before any COMPLETED
+close.
+
+```bash
+# --- Standard Play Close (canonical; see standards/rules/play-close.md) ---
+# Path tokens resolved at pre-flight (resolve here if not already):
+#   ltm_project_target  = yq '.ltm.project-target' .garura/core/config.yaml
+#   evidence_base, slug:
+#     project-scoped play : evidence_base="${stm_base}${issue}/evidence/intent/"     ; slug="#${issue}"
+#     product-scoped play : evidence_base="${product_base}_evidence/intent/"          ; slug="${run_ts}"
+evidence_template=$(cat "${ltm_project_target}standards/templates/evidence-file.md")
+delivery_template=$(cat "${ltm_project_target}standards/templates/delivery-report.md")
+ts=$(date -u +%Y%m%d-%H%M%S)
+evidence_dest="${evidence_base}${ts}.md"
+mkdir -p "$(dirname "$evidence_dest")"
+# Session identity stamp (#463) — close phase; start phase ran at pre-flight
+session_stamp=$(python3 scripts/session_stamp.py --phase close \
+    --marker "${stm_base}_kickoff/intent/status/session-stamp-intent.json")
+# Stop-condition gate (#464) — Step C0: this play carries a baked manifest, so the
+# gate is LIVE. Evaluate the Done means here as the close's authoritative input.
+python3 scripts/check_stop_condition.py \
+    --manifest "<play-dir>/stop-condition.yaml" \
+    --base "<working>/" \
+    --out "${stm_base}_kickoff/intent/status/stop-condition-intent.yaml"
+sc_exit=$?   # 0 held · 1 unmet · 2 error
+```
+
+`/intent` is product-scoped: `evidence_base="${product_base}_evidence/intent/"` and
+`slug="${run_ts}"` (one run can save several intents).
+
+**Step C0 — bind the verdict.** `sc_exit == 0` (held) permits `status: COMPLETED`. Anything
+else closes `HALTED` with `exit_reason: stop_condition_unmet` and the evidence's Stop
+Condition section names every unmet clause. A run the person stopped at Step 4 closes HALTED
+with `exit_reason: not_confirmed`; so does a run where the person dropped every intent. An unevaluable verdict is never a pass.
+
+**Step C1 — Write evidence file.** Gated by the resolved `evidence.record` flag (global +
+per-play `evidence.plays.intent`; first match wins, absent ⇒ record). When false, skip the
+write and record `evidence skipped (record=false)` in the report's pointer line. Otherwise
+fill the `evidence-file.md` slots (play `intent`, run_id `intent-${ts}`, the confirmed and
+dropped intent ids and ICE ids, started_at/completed_at, status per C0, exit_reason; artifacts
+produced: the intent pages, the ICE files, each Source's snapshot and `source.md`, every
+`source-manifest-<name>.json`, `intent-draft.yaml`, `intent-check.json`, `answers.yaml`,
+`confirmation.yaml`, `intent-manifest.json`, `guard-report.json`, the model-delta commit sha;
+`in_drive`; step and scenario eval results SE-1…SE-13 / SCE-1…SCE-8; the checkpoint decisions from Step 4
+with the person's exact replies;
+the session identity stamp fields from $session_stamp (#463): session_id, ledger_file,
+ledger_start_offset, ledger_end_offset (null when unresolved — never blocks the close); and
+stop_condition per C0) and write to `$evidence_dest`. Do NOT hand-author the body.
+
+**Step C2 — Render delivery report.** Also render the **Next** line: resolve this play in `standards/rules/pipeline-next.md` and emit `**Next:** /<command> — <why>. Or run /next to see all recommended actions.` (only /next pointer, or omit, when the mapped command is null), per `play-close.md`. Fill the `delivery-report.md` slots and output the
+report: `## intent Delivered — <n> business intents, <m> ICE`, the Run Summary table, the
+Pipeline Steps table from the task DAG, the Artifacts Produced table (the intent pages, the ICE
+files and the Sources), Next Steps (run `/vision` for a confirmed intent; it finds the intent
+and its ICE in the model), and a pointer to
+`$evidence_dest`. Always emitted; never gated.
+
+```bash
+# --- end Standard Play Close ---
+```
